-- [Oxidation Numbers: Assignment & Rules] ans=A medium skill2
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = 'Hydrogen peroxide, \(\text{H}_2\text{O}_2\), is the active ingredient in many household disinfecting solutions. A student assigns each hydrogen an oxidation number of \(+1\) and then, applying the usual rule for oxygen, assigns each oxygen \(-2\). Checking the neutral molecule, the student finds the oxidation numbers sum to \(-2\) rather than \(0\). What is the correct oxidation number of oxygen in \(\text{H}_2\text{O}_2\), and why does the usual assignment fail here?',
  options = '[{"label": "A", "text": "\\(-1\\)"}, {"label": "B", "text": "\\(-2\\)"}, {"label": "C", "text": "\\(+1\\)"}, {"label": "D", "text": "\\(0\\)"}]'::jsonb,
  correct_answer = 'A',
  explanation = 'This is a General Chemistry question that falls under the content category ''Stoichiometry (GC).'' The oxidation-number guidelines assign hydrogen \(+1\) when combined with nonmetals and oxygen \(-2\) in most compounds, but the overriding rule is that the oxidation numbers of all atoms in a neutral molecule must sum to zero. When the default oxygen value conflicts with that constraint, the constraint wins and signals a special case; peroxides such as \(\text{H}_2\text{O}_2\) are exactly that case, with oxygen at \(-1\). (Choice A) is correct: keeping \(\text{H} = +1\) and letting the oxidation number of O be \(x\), the neutral-molecule condition gives \(2(+1) + 2x = 0\), so \(x = -1\); the usual \(-2\) value fails because \(\text{H}_2\text{O}_2\) is a peroxide, in which the two oxygen atoms are bonded to each other, an arrangement the standard \(-2\) rule does not cover. (Choice B) is incorrect: \(-2\) is oxygen''s value in most oxides, but applying it blindly here is precisely what produced the impossible sum of \(-2\) for a neutral molecule, which is the clue that the default rule does not apply. (Choice C) is incorrect: \(+1\) mistakenly hands oxygen the value that belongs to hydrogen, inverting which element is more electronegative; oxygen is more electronegative than hydrogen and cannot take a positive oxidation number here. (Choice D) is incorrect: \(0\) is the oxidation number of oxygen only in the free element \(\text{O}_2\); inside \(\text{H}_2\text{O}_2\) the oxygen atoms are bonded to hydrogen and are not in an elemental state, so guideline 1 does not apply. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the oxidation-number rules together with the neutral-molecule sum constraint and recognize when a conflict signals the peroxide exception rather than reading a single value from memory.',
  difficulty = 'medium',
  cognitive_skill = 2
WHERE id = '8f53589b-eec0-49c6-a14a-e6045445835e';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = '8f53589b-eec0-49c6-a14a-e6045445835e';
