  ('8f53589b-eec0-49c6-a14a-e6045445835e', 'B', 'misconception', 'applies default oxygen = -2 rule without checking the neutral-molecule sum'),
  ('8f53589b-eec0-49c6-a14a-e6045445835e', 'C', 'misconception', 'assigns oxygen the hydrogen value, inverting relative electronegativity'),
  ('8f53589b-eec0-49c6-a14a-e6045445835e', 'D', 'misconception', 'treats bonded oxygen as if it were elemental O2 (oxidation number 0)');

-- [Common Oxidizing & Reducing Agents] ans=A medium skill2
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = 'A clean copper wire is placed in a colorless aqueous solution of silver nitrate. Over time, gray metallic silver deposits on the wire and the solution turns blue, consistent with the reaction \(\text{Cu}(s) + 2\,\text{AgNO}_3(aq) \rightarrow \text{Cu(NO}_3)_2(aq) + 2\,\text{Ag}(s)\). Which species acts as the oxidizing agent, and which acts as the reducing agent?',
  options = '[{"label": "A", "text": "\\(\\text{Ag}^+\\) is the oxidizing agent, and \\(\\text{Cu}(s)\\) is the reducing agent."}, {"label": "B", "text": "\\(\\text{Cu}(s)\\) is the oxidizing agent, and \\(\\text{Ag}^+\\) is the reducing agent."}, {"label": "C", "text": "\\(\\text{Cu}^{2+}\\) is the oxidizing agent, and \\(\\text{Ag}(s)\\) is the reducing agent."}, {"label": "D", "text": "\\(\\text{NO}_3^-\\) is the oxidizing agent, and \\(\\text{Cu}(s)\\) is the reducing agent."}]'::jsonb,
  correct_answer = 'A',
  explanation = 'This is a General Chemistry question that falls under the content category ''Stoichiometry (GC).'' In a redox reaction, the oxidizing agent is the species that is itself reduced (its oxidation number decreases), and the reducing agent is the species that is itself oxidized (its oxidation number increases). Tracking oxidation numbers here: copper goes from \(0\) in \(\text{Cu}(s)\) to \(+2\) in \(\text{Cu}^{2+}\) (oxidized), and silver goes from \(+1\) in \(\text{Ag}^+\) to \(0\) in \(\text{Ag}(s)\) (reduced); nitrate is a spectator ion whose atoms do not change oxidation number. (Choice A) is correct: \(\text{Ag}^+\) is reduced (\(+1 \rightarrow 0\)), so it removes electrons from copper and is the oxidizing agent; \(\text{Cu}(s)\) is oxidized (\(0 \rightarrow +2\)), so it donates electrons and is the reducing agent. (Choice B) is incorrect: this reverses the two roles. The species that is oxidized (\(\text{Cu}\)) is the reducing agent, not the oxidizing agent, and the species reduced (\(\text{Ag}^+\)) is the oxidizing agent, not the reducing agent; confusing "the species oxidized is the oxidizing agent" is the most common error. (Choice C) is incorrect: \(\text{Cu}^{2+}\) and \(\text{Ag}(s)\) are the products, not the reactant species doing the electron transfer; an agent must be a reactant, and here \(\text{Cu}^{2+}\) is produced by oxidation while \(\text{Ag}(s)\) is produced by reduction. (Choice D) is incorrect: nitrate is a spectator ion in which neither nitrogen nor oxygen changes oxidation number, so it is neither agent; assuming the accompanying anion drives the redox is a misconception. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply oxidation-number changes to correctly assign the oxidizing-agent and reducing-agent roles rather than restate a definition.',
  difficulty = 'medium',
  cognitive_skill = 2
WHERE id = '6c30463c-29b3-415c-8ea1-a17499db9a52';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = '6c30463c-29b3-415c-8ea1-a17499db9a52';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('6c30463c-29b3-415c-8ea1-a17499db9a52', 'B', 'reversed_relationship', 'swapping oxidizing agent and reducing agent labels'),
  ('6c30463c-29b3-415c-8ea1-a17499db9a52', 'C', 'misconception', 'naming products instead of reactant species as the agents'),
  ('6c30463c-29b3-415c-8ea1-a17499db9a52', 'D', 'misconception', 'treating the spectator anion as the oxidizing agent');

-- [Oxidation/Reduction & Identifying Agents] ans=A medium skill2
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = 'Zinc metal reacts with hydrochloric acid in a single-displacement reaction: \(\text{Zn}(s) + 2\,\text{HCl}(aq) \rightarrow \text{ZnCl}_2(aq) + \text{H}_2(g)\). Which species acts as the oxidizing agent?',
  options = '[{"label": "A", "text": "\\(\\text{HCl}\\)"}, {"label": "B", "text": "\\(\\text{Zn}\\)"}, {"label": "C", "text": "\\(\\text{Cl}^-\\)"}, {"label": "D", "text": "\\(\\text{Zn}^{2+}\\)"}]'::jsonb,
  correct_answer = 'A',
  explanation = 'This is a General Chemistry question that falls under the content category ''Stoichiometry (GC).'' The oxidizing agent is the species that brings about oxidation of another substance and is itself reduced (its oxidation number decreases). To identify it, you must assign oxidation numbers on both sides and find the element whose oxidation number falls. In \(\text{HCl}\), hydrogen is \(+1\) and chlorine is \(-1\); in the products, \(\text{H}_2(g)\) has hydrogen at \(0\), while chlorine remains \(-1\) in \(\text{ZnCl}_2\), and zinc rises from \(0\) in \(\text{Zn}(s)\) to \(+2\) in \(\text{ZnCl}_2\). (Choice A) is correct: hydrogen (as \(\text{H}^+\) supplied by \(\text{HCl}\)) is reduced from \(+1\) to \(0\), gaining electrons, so \(\text{HCl}\) is the substance reduced and therefore the oxidizing agent. (Choice B) is incorrect: zinc is oxidized from \(0\) to \(+2\), losing electrons, which makes \(\text{Zn}\) the reducing agent, not the oxidizing agent; this reverses the two roles. (Choice C) is incorrect: chloride is a spectator whose oxidation number stays \(-1\) on both sides, so it neither oxidizes nor reduces anything; the electronegativity of chlorine does not make it the oxidant here. (Choice D) is incorrect: \(\text{Zn}^{2+}\) is a product formed by the oxidation of zinc, so it is the oxidized form of the reducing agent rather than a reactant that accepts electrons. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply oxidation-number assignment to a novel reaction and connect the direction of change to the definition of an oxidizing agent.',
  difficulty = 'medium',
  cognitive_skill = 2
WHERE id = '22dac3dd-c1ce-44f6-a7fc-392de5dcccfd';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = '22dac3dd-c1ce-44f6-a7fc-392de5dcccfd';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('22dac3dd-c1ce-44f6-a7fc-392de5dcccfd', 'B', 'reversed_relationship', 'confusing oxidizing agent with reducing agent'),
  ('22dac3dd-c1ce-44f6-a7fc-392de5dcccfd', 'C', 'misconception', 'assuming the electronegative halogen is always the oxidant'),
  ('22dac3dd-c1ce-44f6-a7fc-392de5dcccfd', 'D', 'misconception', 'mistaking the oxidized product cation for the electron acceptor');

-- [Oxidation Numbers: Assignment & Rules] ans=B medium skill2
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = 'Hydrogen peroxide, \(\text{H}_2\text{O}_2\), is a neutral molecule in which hydrogen is bonded only to nonmetal atoms. Applying the standard rules for assigning oxidation numbers, what is the oxidation number of each oxygen atom in \(\text{H}_2\text{O}_2\)?',
  options = '[{"label": "A", "text": "\\(-2\\)"}, {"label": "B", "text": "\\(-1\\)"}, {"label": "C", "text": "\\(+1\\)"}, {"label": "D", "text": "\\(0\\)"}]'::jsonb,
  correct_answer = 'B',
  explanation = 'This is a General Chemistry question that falls under the content category ''Stoichiometry (GC).'' Oxidation numbers are assigned by rules: hydrogen bonded to nonmetals is \(+1\), and the oxidation numbers of all atoms in a neutral molecule must sum to zero. The rule that oxygen is \(-2\) applies "in most compounds" but not universally, so it cannot be applied blindly; peroxides are the named exception, and the numbers must be reconciled with the sum-to-zero constraint. (Choice B) is correct: hydrogen is \(+1\) (bonded to the nonmetal oxygen), and for the neutral molecule \(2(+1) + 2x = 0\), which gives \(2x = -2\) and \(x = -1\) per oxygen. This is exactly the peroxide case, in which an oxygen–oxygen bond forces oxygen to \(-1\) rather than its usual \(-2\). (Choice A) is incorrect: \(-2\) results from applying the default "oxygen is \(-2\)" rule without checking the sum-to-zero constraint; here it would give \(2(+1) + 2(-2) = -2 \neq 0\), so \(-2\) is inconsistent with a neutral molecule and this is precisely the peroxide exception the rule warns about. (Choice C) is incorrect: \(+1\) is hydrogen''s oxidation number in this molecule, mistakenly assigned to oxygen by swapping the two elements'' values. (Choice D) is incorrect: \(0\) treats the two oxygen atoms as though they were an elemental substance because they are bonded to each other, but oxygen is elemental (oxidation number \(0\)) only in \(\text{O}_2\) or \(\text{O}_3\), not when also bonded to hydrogen. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the sum-of-oxidation-numbers rule and recognize that the default \(-2\) value for oxygen fails in a peroxide, rather than mechanically inserting the usual value.',
  difficulty = 'medium',
  cognitive_skill = 2
WHERE id = '871134d9-e5d6-4650-b471-4edd679ebcc4';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = '871134d9-e5d6-4650-b471-4edd679ebcc4';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('871134d9-e5d6-4650-b471-4edd679ebcc4', 'A', 'misconception', 'applies default oxygen = -2 rule without recognizing the peroxide exception'),
  ('871134d9-e5d6-4650-b471-4edd679ebcc4', 'C', 'reversed_relationship', 'assigns oxygen the value that belongs to hydrogen'),
  ('871134d9-e5d6-4650-b471-4edd679ebcc4', 'D', 'misconception', 'treats O-O bonded oxygens as elemental (oxidation number 0)');

-- [Common Oxidizing & Reducing Agents] ans=A medium skill2
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = 'A strip of zinc metal is placed in a blue aqueous solution of copper(II) sulfate. Over time the strip becomes coated with a reddish deposit and the blue color fades, following the single-displacement reaction \(\text{Zn}(s) + \text{CuSO}_4(aq) \rightarrow \text{ZnSO}_4(aq) + \text{Cu}(s)\). In this reaction, which species acts as the oxidizing agent?',
  options = '[{"label": "A", "text": "The \\(\\text{Cu}^{2+}\\) ion, whose copper changes oxidation number from \\(+2\\) to \\(0\\)."}, {"label": "B", "text": "The zinc metal, whose zinc changes oxidation number from \\(0\\) to \\(+2\\)."}, {"label": "C", "text": "The copper metal deposited as product, the reduced form of the transferred element."}, {"label": "D", "text": "The sulfate ion, which shuttles charge between the two metal centers."}]'::jsonb,
  correct_answer = 'A',
  explanation = 'This is a General Chemistry question that falls under the content category ''Stoichiometry (GC).'' The oxidizing agent in a redox reaction is the species that is reduced: it accepts electrons and thereby causes another species to be oxidized. To identify it, assign oxidation numbers to each element and find the one whose oxidation number decreases. Here copper drops from \(+2\) in \(\text{Cu}^{2+}\) to \(0\) in \(\text{Cu}(s)\) (reduced), zinc rises from \(0\) in \(\text{Zn}(s)\) to \(+2\) in \(\text{Zn}^{2+}\) (oxidized), and both sulfur and oxygen in sulfate are unchanged (spectator). (Choice A) is correct: \(\text{Cu}^{2+}\) is the reactant whose oxidation number falls from \(+2\) to \(0\), so it is reduced and is therefore the oxidizing agent that removes electrons from zinc. (Choice B) is incorrect: zinc''s oxidation number rises from \(0\) to \(+2\), meaning zinc is oxidized and thus loses electrons; the species that is oxidized is the reducing agent, not the oxidizing agent, so this reverses the two roles. (Choice C) is incorrect: the copper metal \(\text{Cu}(s)\) is the product formed after reduction has occurred, not the reactant that undergoes reduction; the oxidizing agent must be the species present before electron transfer, namely \(\text{Cu}^{2+}\). (Choice D) is incorrect: sulfate is a spectator ion whose atoms keep the same oxidation numbers throughout, so it neither gains nor loses electrons and cannot serve as the oxidizing agent. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the definition of an oxidizing agent by assigning oxidation numbers to a specific reaction and determining which species is reduced.',
  difficulty = 'medium',
  cognitive_skill = 2
WHERE id = 'e6359fa9-86da-4ac1-afdd-370d68d26752';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = 'e6359fa9-86da-4ac1-afdd-370d68d26752';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('e6359fa9-86da-4ac1-afdd-370d68d26752', 'B', 'reversed_relationship', 'confuses oxidizing agent (reduced) with reducing agent (oxidized)'),
  ('e6359fa9-86da-4ac1-afdd-370d68d26752', 'C', 'partial_truth', 'names the reduction product instead of the reactant that is reduced'),
  ('e6359fa9-86da-4ac1-afdd-370d68d26752', 'D', 'misconception', 'treats a spectator ion as a redox participant');

-- [Common Oxidizing & Reducing Agents] ans=A medium skill2
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = 'The thermite reaction is used to weld railroad rails because it releases enough heat to melt iron. It proceeds according to the balanced equation:

\(2\,\text{Al}(s) + \text{Fe}_2\text{O}_3(s) \rightarrow \text{Al}_2\text{O}_3(s) + 2\,\text{Fe}(l)\)

Which species functions as the reducing agent in this reaction?',
  options = '[{"label": "A", "text": "\\(\\text{Al}(s)\\)"}, {"label": "B", "text": "\\(\\text{Fe}_2\\text{O}_3(s)\\)"}, {"label": "C", "text": "\\(\\text{Al}_2\\text{O}_3(s)\\)"}, {"label": "D", "text": "\\(\\text{Fe}(l)\\)"}]'::jsonb,
  correct_answer = 'A',
  explanation = 'This is a General Chemistry question that falls under the content category ''Stoichiometry (GC).'' In any redox reaction, the reducing agent is the reactant that is oxidized: its oxidation number increases because it donates electrons to another species. To identify it, assign oxidation numbers to each element and find the one whose oxidation number rises going from reactants to products. (Choice A) is correct: aluminum begins as the free element with an oxidation number of \(0\) in \(\text{Al}(s)\) and ends at \(+3\) in \(\text{Al}_2\text{O}_3\). Because its oxidation number increases, aluminum is oxidized and therefore donates electrons, making \(\text{Al}(s)\) the reducing agent. (Choice B) is incorrect: iron in \(\text{Fe}_2\text{O}_3\) has an oxidation number of \(+3\) (each oxygen is \(-2\), so \(2x + 3(-2) = 0\) gives \(x = +3\)) and is reduced to \(0\) in \(\text{Fe}(l)\); a species that is reduced is the oxidizing agent, not the reducing agent, so \(\text{Fe}_2\text{O}_3\) plays the opposite role. (Choice C) is incorrect: \(\text{Al}_2\text{O}_3\) is a product, and an oxidizing or reducing agent must be a reactant that is consumed while transferring electrons; the oxidized aluminum has already given up its electrons by the time this product forms, so \(\text{Al}_2\text{O}_3\) is not an agent. (Choice D) is incorrect: \(\text{Fe}(l)\) is a product formed when iron is reduced (gains electrons), so it is the outcome of reduction rather than a species that supplies electrons; the reducing agent is the reactant that was oxidized, not the reduced product. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the definition of a reducing agent by assigning oxidation numbers to a novel reaction and determining which reactant is oxidized.',
  difficulty = 'medium',
  cognitive_skill = 2
WHERE id = '5d848878-b7ff-417a-b90b-fb679a2520aa';
