-- [Oxidation Numbers: Assignment & Rules] ans=B medium skill2
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = 'Sodium peroxide, \(\text{Na}_2\text{O}_2\), is a neutral ionic solid used as an oxygen source in some breathing apparatus. Sodium, a Group 1 metal, takes its characteristic oxidation number in the compound. What is the oxidation number of each oxygen atom in \(\text{Na}_2\text{O}_2\)?',
  options = '[{"label": "A", "text": "\\(-2\\)"}, {"label": "B", "text": "\\(-1\\)"}, {"label": "C", "text": "\\(0\\)"}, {"label": "D", "text": "\\(+1\\)"}]'::jsonb,
  correct_answer = 'B',
  explanation = 'This is a General Chemistry question that falls under the content category ''Stoichiometry (GC).'' Oxygen is assigned an oxidation number of \(-2\) in most compounds, but the guidelines note an important exception: in peroxides oxygen is \(-1\). Because the identity "peroxide" is given but the oxygen value is not, you must combine the sodium value with the rule that oxidation numbers in a neutral compound sum to zero rather than reflexively applying the default oxygen rule. (Choice B) is correct: sodium, a Group 1 metal, is \(+1\), so the two sodium atoms contribute \(2 \times (+1) = +2\). For the neutral compound the atoms must sum to zero, so the two oxygen atoms must total \(-2\); letting each oxygen be \(x\), \(2(+1) + 2x = 0\) gives \(x = -1\), the value characteristic of a peroxide. (Choice A) is incorrect: \(-2\) comes from applying oxygen''s default oxidation number without recognizing that \(\text{Na}_2\text{O}_2\) is a peroxide; that value would make the atoms sum to \(2(+1) + 2(-2) = -2\), which fails to equal the required zero charge of a neutral compound. (Choice C) is incorrect: \(0\) results from over-applying the idea that a bond between two identical atoms (the O–O bond) is split evenly and contributes zero, but oxidation number must also account for the electrons oxygen pulls from the two electropositive sodium atoms, which makes each oxygen negative. (Choice D) is incorrect: \(+1\) reverses the sign of the correct magnitude, mistakenly assigning oxygen the same \(+1\) value as the sodium cations even though oxygen is far more electronegative and must therefore carry a negative oxidation number. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the oxidation-number guidelines to a peroxide, recognizing that the default oxygen value does not hold and solving the neutral-sum constraint to obtain the correct value.',
  difficulty = 'medium',
  cognitive_skill = 2
WHERE id = '17968c2c-95bf-4372-b242-0280d9340218';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = '17968c2c-95bf-4372-b242-0280d9340218';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('17968c2c-95bf-4372-b242-0280d9340218', 'A', 'misconception', 'applies default rule, ignores special case (peroxide)'),
  ('17968c2c-95bf-4372-b242-0280d9340218', 'C', 'misconception', 'overextends identical-atom-bond-splits-evenly reasoning'),
  ('17968c2c-95bf-4372-b242-0280d9340218', 'D', 'reversed_relationship', 'sign reversal / assigns cation value to anion');

-- [Reaction Types & Classification] ans=A medium skill2
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = 'A strip of solid zinc metal is placed into a beaker of aqueous copper(II) sulfate. Over time the blue color fades, a reddish deposit coats the zinc, and the balanced equation is \(\text{Zn}(s) + \text{CuSO}_4(aq) \rightarrow \text{ZnSO}_4(aq) + \text{Cu}(s)\). After assigning oxidation numbers to every element on both sides, how is this reaction best classified?',
  options = '[{"label": "A", "text": "A single-displacement reaction that is a redox reaction, with zinc oxidized and copper reduced."}, {"label": "B", "text": "A double-displacement reaction that is a redox reaction, with sulfur oxidized and copper reduced."}, {"label": "C", "text": "A single-displacement reaction that is not a redox reaction, because the sulfate ion stays intact throughout."}, {"label": "D", "text": "A combustion reaction that is a redox reaction, with copper oxidized and zinc reduced."}]'::jsonb,
  correct_answer = 'A',
  explanation = 'This is a General Chemistry question that falls under the content category ''Stoichiometry (GC).'' A reaction is classified as oxidation-reduction (redox) when one or more elements change oxidation number, and as single-displacement when one element in solution is replaced by a free element that is itself oxidized. Assigning oxidation numbers here: zinc goes from \(0\) in \(\text{Zn}(s)\) to \(+2\) in \(\text{ZnSO}_4\); copper goes from \(+2\) in \(\text{CuSO}_4\) to \(0\) in \(\text{Cu}(s)\); sulfur stays \(+6\) and oxygen stays \(-2\) inside the unchanged sulfate ion. (Choice A) is correct: free zinc replaces copper in solution (single displacement), and because zinc''s oxidation number rises \(0 \rightarrow +2\) (oxidized) while copper''s falls \(+2 \rightarrow 0\) (reduced), electrons are transferred, making it a redox reaction. (Choice B) is incorrect: the reaction is not double displacement, since a free element (\(\text{Zn}\)) is involved rather than two ionic compounds swapping partners, and sulfur does not change—it remains \(+6\) in the intact sulfate on both sides. (Choice C) is incorrect: although the sulfate ion truly is a spectator that stays intact, zinc and copper both change oxidation number, so electron transfer does occur and the reaction is definitely redox. (Choice D) is incorrect: combustion requires rapid reaction with an oxidant (typically \(\text{O}_2\)) producing heat and light, which is absent here, and the oxidation-number changes are reversed—copper is reduced (\(+2 \rightarrow 0\)) and zinc is oxidized (\(0 \rightarrow +2\)), not the other way around. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply oxidation-number assignments to a novel reaction to determine both the displacement pattern and whether electron transfer occurs.',
  difficulty = 'medium',
  cognitive_skill = 2
WHERE id = 'dfd24d6c-747e-4f64-ba75-ba55675c4ca5';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = 'dfd24d6c-747e-4f64-ba75-ba55675c4ca5';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('dfd24d6c-747e-4f64-ba75-ba55675c4ca5', 'B', 'misconception', 'confuses displacement subtype and invents an oxidation-state change on a spectator ion'),
  ('dfd24d6c-747e-4f64-ba75-ba55675c4ca5', 'C', 'misconception', 'over-relies on the intact-spectator-ion cue to wrongly rule out redox'),
  ('dfd24d6c-747e-4f64-ba75-ba55675c4ca5', 'D', 'reversed_relationship', 'assigns the correct redox label but reverses which element is oxidized vs reduced');

COMMIT;
